<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-05-basic-interaction">
    <title>Basic Interaction</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Now that the ship, lasso, and crystal all spawn, grow, and move
            together, let's create some basic interaction code.
            Since the prize interacts with both the collector and the ship, it
            is a good place to start for adding interaction effects.
            The initial effects will be the crystal disappearing on contact with
            the ship or collection by the lasso, energy/points for collecting
            with the lasso, and damage for the ship when colliding with the
            crystal.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-basic-interaction-collecting-prizes">
        <title>Collecting Prizes</title>

        <p>
            The following changes are all to the prize script.
            First, create a signal for when the prize is collected with a value
            for the score/energy for collecting.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
signal collected(value: int)
        </program>
        <p>
            Add a member variable that is an Array of the lassos (Collector
            Nodes) that the prize is currently touching.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
var _touching_collectors: Array[Node] = []
        </program>
        <p>
            At the end of <c>_process()</c> check to see if a collector is able
            to collect the prize.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func _process(delta: float) -&gt; void:
    
    # ... code for changing sprite image 
    #     and prize value ...

    # call the collection interaction
    check_touching_collectors()
        </program>
        <p>
            The <c>check_touching_collectors</c> function loops through the
            touching_collectors array and checks if any of them are able to
            collect based on the collection constraints.
            And if they are then the prize is removed from the scene and the
            collected energy is emitted.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func check_touching_collectors() -&gt; void:
    for area in _touching_collectors:
        var collector: Collector = area as Collector
        if collector.can_collect(self.global_position):
            emit_signal("collected",value)
            queue_free()
        </program>
        <p>
            There are two companion functions that put the collectors into the
            touching_collectors array.
            These are _on_area_entered which uses the collisionarea2D's area
            entered signal,
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func _on_area_entered(area: Area2D) -&gt; void:
    if area.is_in_group("collector"):
        if area in _touching_collectors:
            return
        _touching_collectors.append(area)

        </program>
        <p>
            and _on_area_exited, which uses the CollisionArea2D's area_exited
            signal,
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func _on_area_exited(area: Area2D) -&gt; void:
    if area.is_in_group("collector"):
        if area in _touching_collectors:
            _touching_collectors.erase(area)
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-basic-interaction-health-impacts">
        <title>Health Impacts</title>

        <p>
            Create a signal for when the prize is hit by the ship, and the
            amount of damage that the ship takes.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
signal hit(value: int)
        </program>
        <p>
            Since the ship is a RigidBody2D, the body_entered signal is used for
            when the ship makes contact with a prize and both destroys the prize
            and damages the ship using the hit signal.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func _on_body_entered(body: Node2D) -&gt; void:
    if body.is_in_group("player"):
        emit_signal("hit",value)
        queue_free()
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-basic-interaction-main-scene">
        <title>Main Scene Updates</title>

        <p>
            The collected and hit signals from prize need to be connected in the
            plant_crystal method in the main scene.j
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func plant_crystal() -&gt; void:
    
    # after the prize has been created
    
    prize.collected.connect(collect)
    prize.hit.connect($Player.take_damage)
    
    # before the prize is added as a child
        </program>
        <p>
            The collect method increases the score by amount and calls Player's
            connect method.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func collect(amount: int):
    score += amount
    $Player.collect()
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-basic-interaction-player-scene">
        <title>Player Scene Updates</title>

        <p>
            Since Player's take_damage and collect functions are called by main,
            let's write them now so that the main scene can run.
            Open up the player script.
            You'll need to add the member variable health and the signal
            wrecked.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
var health: int
signal wrecked
        </program>
        <p>
            Then let's add the take_damage method.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func take_damage(amount: int) -&gt; void:
    health -= amount
    if health &lt; 0:
        health = 0
    if health == 0:
        emit_signal("wrecked")
        </program>
        <p>
            Next let's add the collect method.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func collect() -&gt; void:
    health += 1
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-basic-interaction-player-state-machine">
        <title>Player State Machine</title>

        <p>
            Now that the player has health, it is useful
            to create a set of states for when the Player is 
            active vs. not active. The ship will have 3 states,
            the initial state, the running state, and the wrecked 
            state. These states can be specified by an enum member
            variable.
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
enum State {INIT, RUNNING, WRECKED}
var state : State = State.INIT
        </program>
        <p>
            Then the <c>_ready()</c> method can update the state:
        </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
func _ready() -> void:
    change_state(State.RUNNING)

    ## leave the rest of the function as it was.
        </program>
        <p>
            <c>change_state()</c> is a helper function that updates
            the state enum, and the collision polygon's disabled state.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func change_state(update : State):
    match update:
        State.INIT:
            $CollisionPolygon2D.set_deferred("disabled",true)
        State.RUNNING:
            $CollisionPolygon2D.set_deferred("disabled",false)
        State.WRECKED:
            $CollisionPolygon2D.set_deferred("disabled",true)
    state = update
        </program>
        <p>
            The state is checked in get_input() and 
            the input is only read if the state is RUNNING.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func get_input() -> void:
    
    # after reseting member variables to 0
    if state in [State.INIT,State.WRECKED]:
        return
    # before reading from Input
        </program>
        <p>
            launch() shouldn't happen if it's not RUNNING:
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func launch() -> void:
    if state != State.RUNNING:
        return
    # the rest of the launch function
        </program>
        <p>
            in take_damage, when health is 0
            the state should change to wrecked:
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func take_damage(amount: int) -> void:
    
    # code before this line stays the same
    if health == 0:
        change_state(State.WRECKED)
        emit_signal("wrecked")       </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-basic-interaction-playtesting">
        <title>Play Testing</title>

        <p>
            After adding the basic interaction, run the main scene and test if
            the distance for collection looks good.
            Make sure that the crystal disappears both when the prize is
            collected and when the ship hits it.
            Make sure the ship is disabled after hitting the right amount of
            crystals.
            Try changing values to see if game play improves.
            Think about which values increase or decrease difficulty so that you
            can modify them as players get to higher levels.
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>